WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.090
CYNTHIA THOMAS: Hi.

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.880
This is Cynthia
Thomas, and I'm back

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.090
to give you the latest on
Google Cloud Edge and security

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.030
networking launches that
have recently gone GA.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:09.675
Stick around.

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:12.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.170
Cloud Armor-- what is it?

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.650
It's Google Cloud's DDoS defense
service and web application

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:27.020
firewall that works to protect
your applications and websites.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.270
Cloud Armor protects
your applications

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.570
against DDoS attacks using
the same technology that's

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.830
used to protect
Google's own services.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:39.370
Additionally, a host of security
capabilities are available.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.420
But in this video, we'll focus
on the latest and greatest

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.520
that are now GA.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.680
Cloud Armor policies
have now been

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.790
extended to help you mitigate
the top 10 OWASP web app

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.590
vulnerabilities in an
easy-to-configure fashion.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.520
The new preconfigured WAF rules
include scanner detection,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:04.970
protocol attack, PHP injection,
and session fixation.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.830
The available
preconfigured rules

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.670
are based on OWASP
ModSecurity core rules set.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.860
The rules allow Cloud
Armor to evaluate

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.050
dozens of distinct
traffic signatures

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.380
by referring to
conveniently named rules,

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:23.890
instead of requiring you to
define each signature manually.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.600
They can be tuned to different
sensitivity levels, as well.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.120
Cloud Armor also
launched the capabilities

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.900
to parse JSONs,
filter based on ASN,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.810
and enable verbose logging.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.230
Incoming requests with
JSON as the content type

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.690
often report a
high rate of noise

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.770
and false positive hits from
preconfigured WAF rules.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.920
With this release, you
can now enable Cloud Armor

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.210
to parse the JSON formatted
content of HTTP requests

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.040
on a per-policy basis.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.200
Once enabled, you
can significantly

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.010
reduce the noise and false
positive alerts for workloads

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.410
with requests containing JSON.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:10.400
Another available rule attribute
is filtering based on an ASN.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.140
Cloud Armor lets you allow or
deny incoming requests serviced

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.400
from a specific network operator
defined by their autonomous

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.230
system number.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.720
Finally, you can get
more logging information

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.430
of incoming requests.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.850
By default, event logs
contain the security rule

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.110
that was triggered.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.570
With verbose
logging, Cloud Armor

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:34.940
can generate additional
details in the request

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:38.900
logs to include a snippet of
the offending content that

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.620
triggered the rule.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.520
Next, let's talk about
additions to VPC Service

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.110
Controls, your Google Cloud
service perimeter defense tool.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.890
VPC Service Controls works
to prevent data exfiltration

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.340
and is a hard requirement for
several financial services

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.120
customers.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.220
It defines a service
perimeter that

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.220
lets you protect your
organization's information

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.950
by controlling
access to resources

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.360
and manage services data.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.110
Now VPC Service
Controls has expanded

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.020
the services you can include
in the service perimeter.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.530
It now includes the
Cloud Filestore API.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.830
Filestore is a Google-managed,
network-attached storage

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.010
solution.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:24.630
It works with VMs and
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.880
And with VPC Service
Controls, you

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.480
can now control and protect the
Data Exchange with Filestore.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.120
Finally, let's talk about
VPC Service Controls

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.200
Troubleshooter.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.340
This is a new tool that enables
admins to better understand

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.550
a denial caused by
VPC Service Controls.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.250
Defining your
security perimeter can

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.530
be tricky while
debugging denial errors.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.740
You can now easily
investigate issues

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.880
related to VPC Service Controls
using the Cloud Console.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.670
Use the VPC Service
Controls Troubleshooter

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.870
via the Logs Explorer or from
the VPC Service Controls page.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.960
Once you enter a unique
ID for the logged error,

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.330
you'll be provided with
one of the violation

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.050
reasons for further
insights into the denial.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.210
Very handy for
confirming and defining

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.580
your service perimeters.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:16.240
All right.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.940
Now you're up-to-date on the
latest networking and Edge

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.410
security launches.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.870
Check out the docs linked
below and let us know

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.970
what you think in the comments.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:29.430
Join us next time for
"What's New In Networking."

